Z||000079||EdsB52||08-07-2001||03:11 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<P><BR>  when He has revealed Himself to me<P>  just like He said He would.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"Someplace, somewhere, a preacher was talking about Jesus, and a person stood up and told his peers, "If what this man is saying is not true, then what he is doing doesnt matter - but if what he is saying is true, then nothing else matters"."<P>Ya, I like to call people that believe 'just in case' there is a Jesus; 'Just in casers.' I think everyone that prescribes to Christianity has at least a miniscule shadow of a doubt, but figures it isn't hurting anything to believe, and they're right, it doesn't hurt anything.<P>"everyone has to deal with the issue of religion and the reality of God at some point in their lives - and come to their own conclusions based on what is in their own heart."<P>Absolutely true. With that, I'd like to say that God does exist on earth. Whether you're an atheist, agnostic, or Christian (or other faith) you will most likely deal with the issue of a God, even if to avoid it. God is alive and well on earth and is often the focus of many conversations, like this one here. But just because the entity of God is present on earth doesn't effect whether a God exists after this life. And as to deciding based on their heart, well, maybe based on what's in their head as well or instead. Believing in religion is based entirely on faith. Disbelieving is based either on a lack of faith or an abundance of information in which we place faith. Kind of the same, kind of different.<P>"sooner or later you will realize the world is more than flesh and bones - supernatural things are real - things we dont understand at face value and cant explain with science<P>then you can either pretend you didnt see what you just saw, turn your back on it, or try to find a way to make it fit into your view of the universe"<P>Is that true to everyone? Are you saying that everyone will witness a supernatural event? How about kids that die very young? How about people of limited mental capacity? Kind of an optimistic and presumptuous statement, but very idealistic sounding. And if we can't explain it with science then it's Christianity? I call this the 'Jesus default theory.' Is it possible science is knowledgeable enough yet? Is it possible that it is other than science or religion?<P>"Over the years I experienced things, I learned things, I studied things, and by the time I was 30 I came to believe that Jesus is real, the Bible is an accurate message system written in 66 books by 40 authors over thousands of years, but yet its an integrated means of communication that has a single clear and important message. And its totally different from every other religious text ever written - almost the total opposite of every other religion on earth."<P>I think it's called realizing mortality, which usually occurs at about 30 years old. What do you know about 'every' other religion, based on every other religious text? How many different religious texts have you read? Again, more idealism learned while sitting in a church dropping hard-earned money into the tithing tray that should be spent on Fiero parts.<P>"But as to whether or not it can be proven - yes it can. Jesus said he would reveal Himself to people, that God would reveal Himself to people, now, today, while we are still alive."<P>You know all fifty States have laws against revealing oneself, and if to a minor it can be a felony. Then he would be required to register for eternity as an offender. The proverbial 'potential proof' that isn't quite ready and no one knows when it will be available is what makes religion faith based. Proof and faith are not synonymous. When science is stumped, they admit it and appeal for more information. When Christianity/religion is stumped they defer to "Jesus works in mysterious ways."<P>"And all the skeptics and scientist in the world, all the dinorsaur bones or scientific theories cannot convince me that Jesus does not exist"<P>And that's another distinct difference; science is not out to disprove religion, however religion makes every attempt at defaming science. Science would welcome any evidence of the existence of any God; religion would consider the opposite blasphemy.||||reg||1
Z||000080||EdsB52||08-07-2001||04:26 AM||||"Ed, just continue like you are, parading around the forum and grandstanding with a "Look at Me, I'm smarter then you are attitude" by ripping peoples opinions or views apart like tissue paper and at the same time and in your indirect way, belittling/berating them through your inadvertent mockery because they do not live up to your level of intelligence."<P>I'm guessing you're the only one in the forum with an inferiority complex.<P>"I pointed out how obvious you are in another thread, when my intentions to begin with there was not to get into an argument with you, but to ask you to trim down your posts because they were becoming annoying."<P>Pot calling the kettle black.<P>"I was just wondering how long it would take before you elected to post my email that I sent to you, that I was about to post on the respective thread. However, prior to hitting the SUBMIT button, I elected to email it to you instead and see what you would do with it. You did, exactly what I had anticipated you would do. It was just a matter of timing for you to again, attempt to discredit me. To quote you, "Your so simple and easy to manipulate" and you did exactly what I wanted and was anticipating you to do.'<P>Right, anticipated. What I write in here, like all of us, is fairly predictable. If someone emails me something private and is respectable, even if they disagree with me, I'll keep it private. But if someone acts the fool in private I think it's worthy of publicity and I will post as I have done since I've been in here. In order to manipulate someone you must persuade them to do something they wouldn't already do. With that, you've failed to persuade me to do something I wouldn't have done anyway. <P>"To quote you, "Your so simple and easy to manipulate" and you did exactly what I wanted and was anticipating you to do.'"<P>Hey dirt farmer, it's great to quote people. In fact you can use it to reinforce your opinion by distinguishing their statements from your argument. But when you quote someone, be exact or look like a liar to everyone. The above quote is not what I posted verbatim. What I wrote on the last post of the second page of this thread <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/002791-2.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/002791-2.html</A>    is, "You're so simple and easy to manipulate." I know the difference between 'your' and 'you're,' do you? They call my version a contraction. Even though you will surely call it a technicality, I do not.<P>"Sad to say, by that time the cancer that you are or have become will have either spread and have potentially destroyed members moral around here or forced members to leave at the fear of being criticized by Mr. Ed's over inflated ego of intelligence, should they post something that you seemingly find controversial or can twist to find ambiguity in it to make them either look foolish or like idiots."<P>I don't think the rest of the members are that manipulable, do you? I think they form their own ideas and won't leave a forum they enjoy because others disagree with them. "Moral?" I'm not here to destroy anyone’s religious values. Oh, you mean morale. Hey, stick to words that are no more than 6 letters long and host no more than 2 syllables......oh wait, let me revise that to 3 letters and 1 syllable. I can't make you look foolish, only you can.<P>"I really wonder if the persona you portray here, if you do this directly and randomly in public down in Arizona, i.e., challenging anyone? Or are you just a coward in real life that has found a medium in which he can act bigger then he really is?"<P>I continually challenge my employer when they act irresponsibly. I fight virtually every traffic ticket I receive and win a fair amount of them in court. For the ones I lose in court, I appeal to higher courts up to and including the State Supreme Court. I've challenged 5 teachers on grades I've received and have won 4 of them. The one I lost went to the Dean of the school where he subtly begged me not to take it higher. I dropped it because I was transferring to University and was tired of the fight. Anyway, to answer your question; I generally protest everything to the maximum that I feel is unfair. I'm not a coward.<P>"If you do portray this persona in real life and in public, I really feel sorry for you. Someday, you are going to be without the safety net of the internet that you have come accustomed to and be a little over arrogant where somebody who you may cross isn't going to settle for what you have to say and might just put a cap in your belligerent a@@."<P>That's brilliant. I prefer personal confrontations (debate) to the Internet, however I keep keen my writing skills when I debate through the Internet.<P>"Do I wish this on you? Absolutely not. I wouldn't wish that on anyone, including my worst enemy (to which I have none). Yet, if it does happen someday because you are so sure of yourself, all I can say is you brought it upon yourself."<P>Sure you do. After all I made your hate list. You don't have enemies but you do have a hate list? You're a walking contradiction.<P>"It is apparent that you have absolutely no scruples or consideration for anyone else, other then your egotistical self." <P>That's why you call me a name in virtually every post and the worse thing I write is "dirt farmer."<P>"Sooner or later, on the track that you are going down, it will also become obvious to them as well, as no one can maintain a facade for to long without themselves and their true intentions coming out."<P>Good point, what are yours? Spread illiteracy throughout PFF?<P>"Go ahead Ed, rip this post and any others that I may post in the future. It will take a lot more then you to demoralize me."<P>You're self-demoralizing.||||reg||1
Z||000081||EdsB52||08-07-2001||04:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B> OK Ken... Now I'm getting scared! We argee on something! HAHAHA<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"I wonder about the fact that science has proven that evolution exists. Not saying that we came from apes, but Man has evolved and that dinosaurs have been dated to millions of years before the first man. These questions I think are there to give us a desire to know more, and also to show Faith. The definition of Faith is the belief in something that you cannot prove."<P>Evolution exists, the way I understand it, in every organism. Not the single organism, but within generations of an organism. But it is definitely not proven that we (homo sapien) have evolved from any other species or has it proven exactly where we came from. My faith ends with science where the irrefutable proof ends. I have no faith in the unknown. It would be hypocritical to pooh-pooh religion due to disagreement with blind faith, and then place blind faith in al of science for which there is no irrefutable proof.<P>"I know that I feel better acting as a Christian. If there is no higher reward, no harm done. But if there is I am glad that I will be ready... And I don't feel like I have missed a thing."<P>Oops, there's that 'just in case' philosophy to which I was earlier referring.||||reg||1
Z||000082||EdsB52||08-07-2001||04:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>Damn there are some long posts in here.  <P>Let me just say this.  If you think that Christianity has caused millions of deaths or whatever, you have no idea what your talking about.  True Christianity does not preach any type of violence.  Maybe you should take some classes on the subject before spouting your mouth off.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>As much as I'd like to disagree, I can't. You're right; it's as fallacious to state Christianity has caused deaths, as it is to contend rock music makes people behave criminally, or that guns kill people. Lunatics find their medium of choice in which they celebrate their crazy behavior. Other opportunists collect this information and skew it in a way that fulfills their agenda. The abortion doctor killer in Florida (and many other places), the Brady Bill fanatics, and the coalition to rate movies and rock music are just a few of the examples of these opportunists. What I would say about Christianity and death/war is that more people have used this rationalization than any other, hence leading people to believe people kill in the name of Christ.||||reg||1
Z||000083||EdsB52||08-07-2001||04:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>"I've begun worhipping the sun for a number of reasons. First of all, unlike some other gods I could mention, I can see the sun. It's there for me every day. And the things it brings me are quite apparent all the time: heat, light, food, a lovely day. There's no mystery, no one asks for money, I don't have to dress up, and there's no boring pageantry. And interestingly enough, I have found that the prayers I offer to the sun and the prayers I formerly offered to "God" are all answered at about the same 50-percent rate."<P>-- George Carlin</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Besides, the sun doesn't pass around the tithing dish and tell you that you can't cohabitate!||||reg||1
